{
  "goldensVersion": 1,
  "author": "director",
  "purpose": "Red-team corpus for food-concept exclusion. This is the gate that decides whether FC-1 actually works. Executors may ADD cases; executors may NOT delete or weaken a case to make a gate pass. A case that cannot be satisfied is a stop-and-report event, not a case to remove.",
  "matching": "Each case names a food by a lowercase substring of its catalogue name. The executor resolves it to concrete foodIds at test time. If a pattern matches zero foods in the 14,484-row catalogue, report it as UNRESOLVED — do not silently skip it. UNRESOLVED is information about coverage, not a failure.",
  "notes": [
    "mustExclude captures false negatives: today's substring matching misses these entirely.",
    "mustNotExclude captures false positives: today's substring matching wrongly catches these. These are the classic food-ontology traps and are the reason negativePhrases exist.",
    "viaIngredient cases require recipe ingredient propagation. They cannot pass by name matching alone and are the specific proof that propagation is wired up.",
    "contested cases are flagged for founder/expert decision rather than assumed. Do not guess."
  ],

